WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.940
Hello, everyone!
Welcome to the Langfocus Channel and my name is Paul.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.760
People who are interested in the history of English

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.700
know that english is a Germanic language that developed from

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:11.660
Old English,

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.540
the language of the Anglo-Saxons

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.640
who began invadind and settling

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.100
in Britain in the 5th century.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.480
It's also widely known that the development of English

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.620
was heavily impacted by two periods of

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.780
contact with other groups of people:

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.980
the influence of old norse, during the period of Scandinavian settlement,

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.320
and the influence of french, after the Norman Conquest.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.520
But there was also another period of intense contact.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.860
When the Anglo-Saxons began invading Britain in the year 449,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.580
they encountered the Celts

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.140
who spoke Celtic languages

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.360
and, unlike today, lived throughout the island of Great Britain.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.200
Didn't this major contact also have an impact on English?

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.475
Well, it's typically thought that it didn't.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.560
That's why so few people talk about it

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.600
The traditionally held view is that the Anglo-Saxons massacred the Celts,

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.700
leaving them in only a couple of pockets on the western side of the island.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.780
That would be Wales or Cornwall

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.260
But genetic research, archeological evidence, and some historical records

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.400
indicate that the Anglo-Saxons ruled over the Celts,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.360
intermarried with them, and enslaved some of them,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.800
and ultimately absorved them into the population

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.075
rather than just killing them all or driving them all out.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.340
(But, of course, lots of that happened too.)

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.420
In a situation like that, it's hard to imagine that English

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.540
was not influenced by Celtic,

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.080
especially since Celts must have formed the majority of the population.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.500
When large numbers of people adopt the language of their rulers,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.340
their native language normally functions as a substrate,

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.540
meaning that it leaves its mark on the new language they adopt.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.800
They learn the new language imperfectly,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.880
with interference from their native language.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.500
So, are there any features of English that likely come from a Celtic language substrate?

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.620
If we focus on vocabulary

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:53.900
than no, not really.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.215
There are only a handful of Celtic words in English from that period,

00:01:57.215 --> 00:02:00.165
and honestly, I've never heard anyone use any those words,

00:02:00.165 --> 00:02:01.660
except for "binn".

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.140
There are, however, a couple of curious grammatical features in English

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.680
that seem uncannily similar to features in two Celtic languages:

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.060
Welsh and Cornish.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.140
The first one is: "DO SUPPORT",

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.700
the use of what John McWhorter call "the meaningless do".

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.840
In English, the word "do" is inserted into many yes-or-no questions

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.320
and negative statements,

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.380
like: "Do you drink coffee?"

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.480
Have you ever thought about why "do" appears in that sentence?

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.640
In many languages of the world, you form yes-or-no questions

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.780
by simply saying a statement with raised intonation.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.860
Something like: "You drink coffee?"

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:35.620
in English.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.980
Of course, in English, "You drink coffee?"

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.300
is something people say,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.860
but for a specific purpose:

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.240
to check that they've understood correctly when they hear something surprising.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.600
But in other languages, that's the regular way to ask a question

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.420
with no such special purpose.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:50.780
For example, in Italian:

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.240
"Bevi il caffè?"

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:53.720
This is the same

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:54.500
as the statement

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:55.720
"Bevi il caffè"

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.360
but with raised intonation

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.240
In some languages, there is a question marker that goes before or after the sentence.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.200
For example,

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.220
in Standard Arabic

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:04.960
you might add

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.800
"hal", before the sentence.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.420
"Hal tashrubu al-qahwa"

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.180
Or, you can say it without the question marker

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.740
so it's the same as the statement,

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.280
but with rising intonation:

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.780
"Tashrubu al-qahwa?"

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.820
And there are other question-forming constructions like:

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:20.800
"Est-ce que ~"

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.920
in French. Litteraly: "Is it that"

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.720
"Est-ce que tu bois du café?"

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.840
Or more formally, you might use inversion instead:

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.060
"Bois-tu du café?"

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.920
But none of them use the equivalent of the verb "do",

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.220
except for two languages:

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:34.660
Welsh and Cornish.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.280
Welsh doesn't use the "meaningless do" in the present tense, but it does in the past tense.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.940
For example: "Nest ti yfed coffi bore yma?"

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.320
Word for word it's:

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.900
Did - you - drink - coffee - the - morning - this

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.240
Notice that this word: "Nest"

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.600
is the past tense of the verb meaning "do".

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.780
It's also used in negatives in English.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:54.640
For example:

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.180
"I don't drink coffee".

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.860
In many other languages, a simple negation word would be added,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.180
something like: "I not drink coffee".

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.480
For example, in Hebrew:

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.820
"Ani lo shoteh qafeh"

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.560
Again, no other languages in the world use the equivalent of the verb "do"

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.640
as an auxiliary in a negative sentence

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:13.860
except fooor

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.020
Welsh and Cornish!

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:16.480
Here's an example in Welsh

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.820
"Nes i ddim yfed coffi bore yma"

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.140
This means "I didn't drink coffee this morning"

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.380
Word for word it's:

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.320
Did - I - not - drink - coffee - the - morning - this

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.300
As we saw before, this word, "Nes"

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.440
is the past tense of the verb meaning "do".

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.980
But again, in Welsh, the verb "do" is only used as an auxiliary in the past tense.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.900
In English we also sometimes use the verb "do" as an auxiliary in affirmative sentences

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:43.560
in order to show emphasis,

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.700
like: "I did wash the dishes!"

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.880
something you might say if your spouse asks:

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.580
"Why didn't you wash the dishes?"

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.460
In Welsh and Cornish, the equivalent of "do" is used generally,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.060
not only to show emphasis.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:56.560
For example, in Welsh:

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.080
"Nes i olchi'r llestri"

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:58.920
Word for word it's:

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.280
Did - I - wash - the - dishes

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.260
This could mean "I DID wash the dishes!", with emphasis,

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.140
or simply "I washed the dishes", without emphasis.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.420
So that might seem like a weak link between English and Celtic

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.740
because in English, it's only used for emphasis,

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.020
but it used to be used in the regular affirmative in English too.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.720
That means it was used without adding emphasis.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.595
it was just a normal way to express the present tense.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.560
Like these examples, written in Early Modern English (EME):

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:26.880
"Doubt that the sun 'doth' move"

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:27.420
and

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.020
"The serpent that 'did' sting thy father's life"

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.560
These are lines from Shakespeare's Hamlet.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.380
Neither of them are emphatic.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.060
By the year 1700,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.440
this usage had fallen out of use in English.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.120
But until then, English resembled Celtic a little more than it does now.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.000
Could these similarities be mere coincidence?

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.520
Well, that'd be a reasonable assumption if "do support" occured in various languages

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:50.260
throughout the world.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.380
But as far as we know, it only occurs in Welsh, Cornish and English.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.460
And since English was superimposed onto a Celtic speaking land,

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.260
the same land where Cornish and Welsh developed,

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.580
then it makes sense that Celtic is the source of that feature in English.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:09.060
Another odd feature of English is the exclusive use of the ~ing form,

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.640
the present continuous form,

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.820
to refer to an action in the present moment.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.201
If we think about what the present continuous really expresses in English,

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:19.600
it is, in a sense, the only true present tense.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.380
To talk what about what you're doing in this moment right now,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:23.600
you say something like:

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.220
"I am working out at the gym"

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.040
"I work out at the gym"

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.880
is not something a native speaker would ever say to talk about the present moment.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:32.720
That refers to general times,

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.000
to something you do on your regular basis.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.840
So, how is English special in this reguard?

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:41.240
After all, lots of languages have a present continuous form, don't they?

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.660
The difference is that in other languages

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.320
the present continuous form is used to emphasize

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.140
that the action is ongoing in the present moment.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:52.060
But you can still use the simple present, and it's correct,

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:53.580
tt just lacks emphasis.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.280
For example, in Spanish:

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:57.160
"Yo estoy comiendo"

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:58.480
meaning "I am eating".

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:02.360
This form emphasizes that the action is happening right now.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.740
But it's also fine to say: "Yo como", which is the simple present form

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.300
How about in Welsh and Cornish?

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:14.400
They're similar to English, in that they use the equivalent of the English progressive form

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.820
to indicate an action in the present moment.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:19.340
One difference is that they include a preposition.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.720
For example, in Welsh:

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:22.360
"Mae o yn rhedeg"

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:23.740
which means "He is running".

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:24.960
Word for word it's:

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.740
Is - he - in - running

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.560
So, literally, it's more like "He is in running"

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.620
In English, there used to be a preposition like this too,

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:34.280
like "I am on running",

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:36.660
which gradually morphed into "I am a running",

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:39.180
and then the preposition was lost entirely:

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.220
"I am running"

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:44.420
If we compare the older English structure to the current one in Welsh and Cornish,

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.420
it's more similar than the current form.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:48.520
In Old English we can say this:

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.700
"Ic eom on huntunʒe"

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:52.720
That's like saying "I am on hunting"

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:55.240
rather than "I'm hunting", in Modern English.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.220
There is more to it than that.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.360
The Celtic progressive is formed with the equivalent of the verb "to be"

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:05.640
followed by a preposition and a verbal noun, rather than a participle.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:09.180
In English there were originally two ways to form the progressive:

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.760
a participle ending with ~ende

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:12.460
For example:

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.220
"Ic eom huntiende"

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:15.680
like "I am hunting";

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.700
and a verbal noun structure

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:19.760
"Ic eom on huntunge"

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.620
Other Germanic languages have retained their equivalents of the ~ende participle,

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:28.920
but in English it came to be replaced by the verbal nounwith ~ing

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.440
which now functions as both the verbal noun and the participle.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:35.700
So English lost the form that resembled Celtic less

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:37.900
and replaced it with the form that closely

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:38.960
resembled Celtic.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.080
That much is clear.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:44.340
The question is: did this happen due to Celtic influence, or some other cause?

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:47.160
The case for Celtic influence seems quite convincing.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:50.520
People who question the Celtic influence hypothesis

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:54.360
usely point out that the contact between Anglo-Saxons and the Celts

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.740
took place hundreds of years before those features started to appear in written English.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:04.100
For the most part it wasn't until the Middle English period that these Celticisms started to appear,

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:09.600
and for hundreds of years, Old English was written without any real indication of a Celtic substrate.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:13.740
If the Celts had influenced English, then why didn't it affect Old English?

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.600
Well, that could be because of the difference between the spoken language and the written language.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:22.680
Old English as we know it, is the written literary language of those days,

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:26.180
and was certainly much more conservative than the spoken language.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:29.580
Celticisms may have been in common use amongst the common people

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:31.880
while elite scribes saw those innovations

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:32.700
as vulgar

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.400
and continued to write Old English

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:36.560
pretty much the same way it had been always been written.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:41.760
Most people were illiterate, so it was highly unlikely that they felt the need to speak like books

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:43.760
or could have, even if they had wanted to.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:49.340
But after the Norman Conquest, French became the language of the elite for hundreds of years

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.720
and written English lost its prestige

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:57.420
It's likely that when English later regained its status, it was free to be written as it was really spoken

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:01.360
because literary Old English had been left unused for a long time.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:03.080
So, the Celtic elements

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:05.380
that had been part of spoken English all along

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.900
were now suddenly part of the written language too.

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:09.720
So, what do I think?

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:13.900
Well, I'm quite convinced that Celtic must've had some influence on English,

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:16.960
and these celticisms that we looked at are quite compelling.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.340
I do have a couple of reservations though.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:22.860
First: while Welsh uses "do support" in the past tense

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:25.000
it does not use it in the present tense

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.600
which makes its link to English seem weaker.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.960
English doesn't have just "did support", it has "do support".

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.420
Also, there are other ways to express the past tense in Welsh.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.380
Sentences with "do support" can be used for both emphatic and non-emphatic sentences,

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:41.600
but they don't have to be used for either.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:44.760
In English, "do" is required in many contexts.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:46.840
Another reservation I have

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:50.920
is that while English has both the present progressive and the simple present tense

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:53.300
that is used for statements about general time

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:55.120
as well as for stative verbs,

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:59.140
Welsh doesn't use different forms to distinguish between general time

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:00.780
and an action in the present moment.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:02.340
So, this makes me wonder:

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.840
is it really a progressive form, or is it simply the all purpose present tense,

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:10.260
and just structurally resembles the English present progressive?

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.860
Of course nobody is saying that Modern Welsh influenced English

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:18.100
but rather that an early form of Welsh and other Celtic languages influenced English.

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.320
So I guess it's normal that the links would have some weaknesses.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:26.240
Some people are bound to ask why this video focuses on Welsh as a point of comparison

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.480
What about Irish and Scottish Gaelic?

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:34.800
Well, Gaelic developed in Ireland, so it wasn't Gaelic that the Anglo-Saxons had the most contact with,

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:36.680
but rather Brythonic languages

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:39.020
the precursors of Welsh and Cornish.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:43.220
And Welsh and Cornish existed alongside English as they developed.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:45.020
So, what about Cornish?

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:47.540
Well, Cornish isn't widely spoken anymore

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:48.040
and, in fact

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.580
it's not spoken as a native language at all anymore.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.840
So it's difficult to find information on it

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:57.600
and it's difficult to find Cornish speakers who can help with the sentences in a video like this.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:11:59.360
The Question of the Day:

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:02.300
What do you think of the similarities between Celtic and English?

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.580
Did they convince you that Celtic languages have influenced English?

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:07.920
Leave your reactions in the comments down below.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:10.940
A very special thanks goes out to my Patreon supporters,

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:12.740
esspecially my top tier patreons

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:14.220
who are right here on the screen.

